---
source: crates/rumps-query/tests/scripts.rs
description: "; Test: Subscript type as subscript key\n; The `Subscript` union type can be used directly as a subscript key,\n; enabling seamless piping of `@order` results into `@get` without coercions.\n\nfun main() {\n  ; Set up test data with mixed subscript types\n  @set data{1} \"one\"\n  @set data{2} \"two\"\n  @set data{3} \"three\"\n  @set data{\"a\"} \"alpha\"\n  @set data{\"b\"} \"beta\"\n\n  ; Use @order result directly as subscript (no read coercion needed)\n  let first : Subscript = @order data{}!\n  write :display(first)\n  write @get data{first}!\n\n  ; Chain through all keys using loop\n  let vals = loop { key: @order data{}, acc: [] } (st, cont) => {\n    match st.key {\n      Option.None => st.acc\n      Option.Some(k) => {\n        ; k is Subscript; use directly in @get and @order\n        let v = @get data{k}!\n        cont({\n          key: @order data{k},\n          acc: Array.concat(st.acc, [v])\n        })\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  write :display(vals)\n\n  ; Explicit Subscript annotation works\n  let sub : Subscript = 42\n  @set x{sub} \"works\"\n  write @get x{sub}!\n\n  write :display(lookup(1))\n  write :display(lookup(\"a\"))\n\n  write \"Subscript type test complete\"\n}\n\n; Function that takes Subscript parameter\nfun lookup (key: Subscript) -> Option[Storable] {\n  @get data{key}\n}\n"
---
1
one
[ "one", "two", "three", "alpha", "beta" ]
works
Option.Some("one")
Option.Some("alpha")
Subscript type test complete
